.\" robodr-uplink(1). Source of truth: the USAGE text in
.\" crates/robodr-export/src/bin/robodr-uplink.rs, the crate README and
.\" docs/contracts/robodr.ingest-api.md. Keep them in sync.
.TH ROBODR\-UPLINK 1 "2026-10-26" "RoboDR 0.1.0-alpha.1" "RoboDR Manual"
.SH NAME
robodr\-uplink \- send RoboDR findings to the SOC ingest API over TLS
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B robodr\-uplink
.B \-\-url
.I URL
.B \-\-key\-file
.I FILE
.B \-\-ca\-bundle
.I PEM
.RB [ \-\-spki\-sha256
.IR HEX ]...
.B \-\-state\-dir
.I DIR
.RB ( \-\-spool
.I DIR
|
.B \-\-diode\-out
.IR DIR )
.RB [ \-\-max\-lines
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-max\-body\-bytes
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-connect\-timeout\-ms
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-request\-timeout\-ms
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-max\-attempts
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-backoff\-base\-ms
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-backoff\-max\-ms
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-refused\-backoff\-max\-s
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-exit\-when\-stuck\-s
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-dead\-letter\-max\-bytes
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-dead\-letter\-keep
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-dead\-letter\-max\-age\-s
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-done\-max\-age\-s
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-done\-max\-count
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-upgrade\-data\-source
.IR LABEL ]
.RB [ \-\-upgrade\-topic
.IR TOPIC ]
.RB [ \-\-interval\-ms
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-once " | " \-\-check ]
.br
.B robodr\-uplink reinject
.B \-\-state\-dir
.I DIR
.RB ( \-\-spool
.I DIR
|
.B \-\-diode\-out
.IR DIR )
.B \-\-dead\-letter
.IR FILE ...
.RB [ \-\-codes
.IR CODE , CODE ...]
.RB [ \-\-max\-record\-bytes
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.br
.B robodr\-uplink \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
Producer of the
.B robodr.ingest
HTTP API, contract v1 (the AutoSecOps
.I /robot\-edr/v2/ingest
endpoint; docs/contracts/robodr.ingest\-api.md in the source tree). It reads
either a connected site's
.BR robodr\-agentd (8)
spool
.RB ( \-\-spool ,
on the robot or sensor box) or the
.BR robodr\-diode\-unpack (1)
importer's
.IB DIR /<batch>/records.jsonl
.RB ( \-\-diode\-out ,
on the SOC side of an air\-gapped site), re\-validates every finding exactly
as
.BR robodr\-kafka\-publish (1)
does, and POSTs NDJSON batches
.RB ( {"value":"<finding>","sig":...}
per line, the value byte for byte) to
.IB URL /robot\-edr/v2/ingest\fR.
A per\-finding signature
.RB ( robodr\-finding\-sig ,
contract robodr.findings \(sc2a) found in the spool record is carried as the
line's
.BR sig ;
findings that crossed a diode have none
.RB ( "sig":null ).
.PP
Each batch is signed with the Ed25519 key file (header
.BR X\-RoboDR\-Signature ,
contract \(sc2.2) and carries a batch id derived from the input positions
of its lines and from its body: re\-sending the same records after a crash
reuses the same id, so the server answers with its stored response
.RB ( "replayed":true )
instead of processing them again. The batch in flight is saved in the state
directory before it is sent.
.PP
Delivery is at\-least\-once: a record leaves the input
.RB ( ready/
\(->
.BR acked/ ,
or the diode batch's committed line count) only after a
.B 200
response names it accepted or rejected; rejected lines go to
.IB DIR /dead\-letter.jsonl
with the server's code (and, with
.BR \-\-spool ,
to the spool's
.BR rejected/ ).
A record that fails the local validation is dead\-lettered with code
.B local
and never sent; so is a spool file that cannot be read (I/O error such as
EACCES or EIO, 3 attempts), with the error as its reason (moved to
.BR rejected/ ,
kept for a re\-drive) instead of failing every run. Network errors,
.BR 408 ,
.B 429
and
.B 5xx
are retried with the same body and batch id and a fresh timestamp and
signature, with exponential backoff and jitter, never sooner than
.BR Retry\-After ,
also when it came with the run's last attempt (the next run waits for it).
.B Retry\-After
is honoured in full up to 24 hours; a larger value is treated as 24 hours
(contract \(sc7).
A
.B 413
halves the batch;
.BR 400 ,
.BR 401 ,
.B 409
and
.B 415
stop the run without acknowledging anything (the batch stays pending and
is re\-sent, under the same id, by the next run). A
.B 200
that does not account for every line of the batch acknowledges nothing.
Delivery is in order: a batch the server keeps refusing blocks every later
record until the cause is fixed (see
.BR "BLOCKED QUEUE" ).
.PP
TLS is mandatory: rustls (ring provider; TLS 1.3 and 1.2), the server
certificate verified against
.B \-\-ca\-bundle
only (no system or built\-in roots), the URL host as the server name, and
optionally SHA\-256 SPKI pins checked in addition. There is no option that
disables verification. Plain
.B http://
is accepted only to a loopback IP literal (127.0.0.0/8,
.BR [::1] ;
not the name
.BR localhost ),
e.g. a local TLS\-terminating proxy, and then without
.BR \-\-ca\-bundle .
No HTTP proxy, no redirects, one request per connection.
.PP
Without
.B \-\-once
it sends every
.B \-\-interval\-ms
and, after a failed run, waits with exponential backoff from
.B \-\-interval\-ms
up to
.B \-\-backoff\-max\-ms
after transient failures (network,
.BR 408 ,
.BR 429 ,
.BR 5xx )
or up to
.B \-\-refused\-backoff\-max\-s
after anything else (refusals, a malformed
.BR 200 ,
local errors), and never less than the server's
.BR Retry\-After .
Each run that did something prints a JSON report on stdout. Progress,
retries and every failed run are logged on stderr; the state of the queue
is in
.IB DIR /uplink\-status.json
and in the systemd status line
.RB ( STATUS= ,
.BR "systemctl status robodr\-uplink" ).
.PP
One process per state directory: the service holds an exclusive lock on
.IB DIR /uplink.lock
for its whole lifetime; a second service, a
.B \-\-once
run or
.B reinject
on the same directory exits with status 1 and says so.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-url " URL"
The AutoSecOps API root
.RB ( https://HOST[:PORT][/PATH] ,
e.g.
.BR https://soc.example.org/api );
.B /robot\-edr/v2/ingest
is appended. No user info, query or fragment.
.TP
.BI \-\-key\-file " FILE"
The sender key: an Ed25519 secret key file made by
.B robodr\-blackbox keygen
(no
.BR \-\-key\-id :
the ingest API identifies keys by the derived
.B ed25519:<16 hex>
id). Mode 0600, owned by the caller or root. Register its public half
.RI ( FILE .pub)
in AutoSecOps as the robot's
.B uplink
key (connected site) or as a
.B relay
key (SOC side of a diode); the role is decided by the server.
.TP
.BI \-\-ca\-bundle " PEM"
The CA certificate(s) the server certificate must chain to (required for
.BR https:// ).
.TP
.BI \-\-spki\-sha256 " HEX"
SHA\-256 (64 hex digits) of the server certificate's DER
SubjectPublicKeyInfo; repeatable (a key rotation lists the current and the
next key). When given, the server key must match one of them, in addition to
the CA verification. Compute it with
.br
.B "openssl x509 \-in server.pem \-pubkey \-noout | openssl pkey \-pubin \-outform der | sha256sum"
.TP
.BI \-\-state\-dir " DIR"
State file
.RB ( uplink\-state.json ),
done markers
.RB ( \-\-diode\-out )
and dead letters. One state directory per input.
.TP
.BI \-\-spool " DIR\fR | " \-\-diode\-out " DIR"
The input (exactly one).
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-lines " N"
Lines per batch, 1..1000 (default 1000, the contract maximum).
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-body\-bytes " N"
Body bytes per batch, 1048576..4194304 (default 4194304, the contract
maximum).
.TP
.BI \-\-connect\-timeout\-ms " N\fR, " \-\-request\-timeout\-ms " N"
Name resolution and TCP connect (per address; default 10000), and the
whole exchange after it: TLS handshake, request, response (default 60000).
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-attempts " N"
Requests per batch per run (default 6); then the run fails and the batch
stays pending.
.TP
.BI \-\-backoff\-base\-ms " N\fR, " \-\-backoff\-max\-ms " N"
Backoff before retry
.IR k :
a random time in [d/2, d], d = min(max, base \(mu 2^(k\-1)) (defaults 500
and 60000).
.TP
.BI \-\-refused\-backoff\-max\-s " N"
Backoff cap between runs after a non\-transient failure (default 900:
under 200 refused requests a day; each
.B 401
is an audit\-log entry on the server). 1..604800.
.TP
.BI \-\-exit\-when\-stuck\-s " N"
Exit with status 3 once runs have failed for
.I N
seconds in a row in this process (default: never), so the unit shows as
failed and restarts. Without it, a fixed registration resumes delivery with
no restart.
.TP
.BI \-\-dead\-letter\-max\-bytes " N\fR, " \-\-dead\-letter\-keep " N"
Rotate
.B dead\-letter.jsonl
before an append would take it past
.I N
bytes (default 67108864, at least 65536; 0 = never rotate), keeping
.I N
rotated files
.RB ( dead\-letter.jsonl.1
newest; default 4). Deduplication reads the rotated files too. Rotation only
renames; after each successful run a rotated file beyond
.I keep
is deleted once none of its entries is still needed for deduplication (its
diode batch is done or left the importer output, its spool file left
.IR ready/ ,
its re\-injected generation is done); otherwise it is held for a later run.
Deleted dead letters can no longer be re\-injected.
.TP
.BI \-\-dead\-letter\-max\-age\-s " N"
Also delete rotated dead\-letter files older than N seconds, by the same rule
(0 or absent = no age bound).
.TP
.BI \-\-done\-max\-age\-s " N\fR, " \-\-done\-max\-count " N"
Delete
.B done/
markers older than N seconds (default 2592000, 30 days; 0 = keep) or beyond
the N newest, but never while their batch is still in the importer output.
.TP
.BI \-\-upgrade\-data\-source " LABEL\fR, " \-\-upgrade\-topic " TOPIC"
An unsigned contract 1.x record is upgraded to contract 2.0 with this
.B data_source
(default
.BR robodr )
and topic (default
.BR robodr.findings )
before it is sent; a signed one is dead\-lettered ("legacy 1.x signed
record: re\-sign required").
.TP
.BI \-\-interval\-ms " N"
Loop interval, > 0 (default 2000).
.TP
.B \-\-once
One pass, then exit.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Site acceptance, without sending a finding: TLS with the CA bundle, pins and
CRLs; the ingest endpoint (a request with valid headers and an empty body,
which the ingest API refuses with
.B 400 empty_batch
before authentication and before anything is stored); and, for a
.B \-\-spool
(uplink) key, its registration through a read\-only, authenticated fleet
request
.RB ( "GET .../robot\-edr/v2/fleet/ring" :
.B 200
or
.B 404 no_ring
pass,
.B 401
names the reason). A relay key
.RB ( \-\-diode\-out )
and a SOC without the fleet API skip the key step, said so on the output.
Takes no lock and writes nothing, so it runs next to the service. Exit 0
passed, 1 failed.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-max\-retry\-after\-s
Removed (usage error): a cap could only retry sooner than the server asked.
.SH REINJECT
.B robodr\-uplink reinject
re\-queues dead\-lettered records after the cause was fixed (a robot or key
registered, a site scope corrected, a finding key added). It reads the
given dead\-letter files (repeat
.B \-\-dead\-letter
for the rotated ones), selects the entries whose code is in
.B \-\-codes
(default
.BR unknown_robot ,
.BR robot_mismatch ,
.BR site_mismatch ,
.BR missing_finding_signature ,
.BR bad_finding_signature ),
re\-reads each record from the original input (the spool's
.BR rejected/ ,
the diode batch's
.BR records.jsonl ,
or an earlier re\-injection) or, if that is gone, from the dead letter's
copy, and writes them as one generation under
.IB DIR /reinject/ .
The uplink sends a generation after its regular input, as the stream
.BI reinject: generation\fR,
under
.B new
batch ids (the old id would only return the server's stored rejection).
Every record is validated again before sending. Idempotent: a dead letter
already re\-injected is skipped. A record rejected again is dead\-lettered
under
.BI source " reinject:<generation>"
and can be re\-injected again later. Stop the service first (it holds the
lock):
.PP
.nf
systemctl stop robodr\-uplink
robodr\-uplink reinject \-\-state\-dir /var/lib/robodr/export/uplink \e
    \-\-spool /var/lib/robodr/spool/agentd \e
    \-\-dead\-letter /var/lib/robodr/export/uplink/dead\-letter.jsonl \-\-dry\-run
systemctl start robodr\-uplink
.fi
.PP
It prints a JSON report
.RB ( generation ,
.BR queued ,
.BR from_input ,
.BR from_dead_letter ,
.BR already_queued ,
.BR other_code ,
.BR malformed ,
.BR unavailable ).
Run it as the service user
.RB ( robodr ).
.SH BLOCKED QUEUE
A refused batch is retried on the long backoff and blocks the later
records. Find the cause in
.B last_error
of
.IB DIR /uplink\-status.json
(or the
.B STATUS=
line):
.TP
.B 401 unknown_key
The key is not registered (or revoked, or registered with the
.B finding
role): register
.IR FILE .pub
in AutoSecOps with the right role.
.TP
.B 401 stale_timestamp
The clock is off by more than 300 s: fix NTP.
.TP
.B 401 bad_signature
Key file and registered public key differ: re\-register.
.TP
.BR "400" ", " "409" ", " "415" ", single\-line " "413" ", malformed " 200
A producer/server mismatch or bug (or server limits below the contract's):
upgrade or fix the server; do not delete the state file (the pending batch
would be re\-cut, and its records reported as duplicates at best).
.PP
Delivery resumes by itself once fixed (at the next attempt,
.B next_attempt_at_ms
in the status file; restart the unit to retry at once).
.SH FILES
.TP
.IB DIR /uplink\-state.json
Committed line count per diode batch and the pending batch (batch id,
input positions, body SHA\-256, the server's result once received).
.TP
.IB DIR /uplink\-status.json
Written after every run (atomically):
.B state
.RB ( ok ,
.B retrying
after transient failures,
.B blocked
after refusals and other failures that need a fix),
.BR last_success_at_ms ,
.BR last_delivery_at_ms ,
.B last_error
.RB ( kind ,
.BR status ,
.BR code ,
.BR message ,
.BR retry_after_s ),
.BR consecutive_failures ,
.B stuck_since_ms
(start of the failure streak, kept across restarts while the same batch is
pending),
.BR pending_batch_id ,
.BR pending_lines ,
.BR pending_failed_runs ,
.B backlog
.RB ( spool_ready
or
.BR diode_batches ,
.BR reinject_generations ),
.BR next_attempt_at_ms ,
.BR dead_letter_malformed ,
.B quarantined_unreadable
(spool files this process could not read, see below) and the delivery totals. Times are ms since the epoch.
.TP
.IB DIR /uplink.lock
The state directory's lock (never removed; harmless when stale: the kernel
releases it when the process dies).
.TP
.IB DIR /reinject/ <generation> /
A re\-injected generation
.RB ( records.jsonl ,
.BR origin.jsonl ,
.B done
once fully acknowledged; kept as the re\-injection ledger).
.TP
.IB DIR /dead\-letter.jsonl
.RB { source ,
.BR position ,
.BR code ,
.BR reason ,
.BR batch_id ,
.BR record ,
.BR at_ms }
per line, deduplicated on
.RB ( source ,
.BR position )
over the current and rotated files
.RB ( dead\-letter.jsonl.1 ...).
Appends are fsynced; a line torn by a crash is closed before the next
append and counted as malformed (reported in the run's JSON report and the
status file).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success
.RB ( \-\-once ).
.TP
.B 1
Runtime error with
.B \-\-once
or
.BR reinject ,
the key file or CA bundle could not be loaded, or the state directory is
locked by another process.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error (including a refused
.B http://
URL).
.TP
.B 3
Runs failed for longer than
.BR \-\-exit\-when\-stuck\-s .
.SH SYSTEMD UNIT
The package ships
.B robodr\-uplink.service
(installed disabled) for a connected site: it runs on the unit or sensor box
as user
.B robodr
with
.BR "\-\-spool /var/lib/robodr/spool/agentd" ,
state in
.IR /var/lib/robodr/export/uplink ,
the key
.I /etc/robodr/keys/uplink.key
handed over as a systemd credential and the CA bundle
.IR /etc/robodr/uplink\-ca.pem ;
the URL and extra flags come from
.I /etc/robodr/uplink.env
.RB ( ROBODR_UPLINK_URL ,
.BR ROBODR_UPLINK_ARGS ).
The unit denies every IP address; the drop\-in example
.I 50\-soc.conf.example
allows the SOC address. DNS servers are not reachable from the unit unless
allowed there: map the URL's host name in
.IR /etc/hosts .
It conflicts with
.B robodr\-spool\-export.service
and
.B robodr\-kafka\-publish.service
(one consumer per spool).
